hi everyone and welcome to biology  professor today we're going to talk  about the three different states of  matter  remember that matter is what makes up  everything around you matter is anything  that occupies space and has the mass and  all different kinds of matter can exist  in three possible States depending on  the temperature and pressure that the  matter is at those states are solid  liquid and gas now if you're interested  in learning more about the different  types of matter please see my video on  classification of matter but now let's  talk about these three different states  I have drawn here images of what water  molecules look like in their solid  configuration what we know of as ice in  their liquid configuration so liquid  water and in their gaseous configuration  water vapour now we can see that they  certainly have some differences let's  start by talking about shape and volume  in solid water like an ice cube both the  shape of the ice cube and the volume of  the ice cube are fixed liquid water  however still has a fixed volume meaning  it takes up the same amount of space but  its shape is not fixed this is because  liquid water takes on the shape of its  container so let's say that you have a  glass of water the water takes the shape  of that glass if you then pour the water  on the floor or pour the water into a  vase or pour the water into a shallow  bowl in all of these instances the water  will take  on a different shape with gases like  water vapor both the shape and the  volume are unfixed think about water  that is coming from a boil so you've got  boiling water and water vapor is  evaporating off the top of the boiling  water that water vapor will move outward  expanding until it fills the entire room  so water vapor like all gases it has a  shape and volume that is not fixed  meaning it takes on both the shape and  the volume of its container another good  example of this is a balloon if you  think about blowing up a balloon the gas  that you put inside the balloon will  take on the shape and the volume of the  balloon now let's talk about two other  kinds of characteristics that is the  speed of particles how quickly they're  moving and the proximity of particles  how close or far apart they are from  each other we'll talk about speed of  particles first and gasses particles are  moving very rapidly on the other end of  the spectrum the speed of particles in a  solid is quite slow and liquids the  speed of their particles are at more of  an intermediate range belongs this  continuum now let's take a second to  talk about whether or not it's possible  for particles to completely stop when we  think about the particles of a solid  like an ice cube  or a chair or a mark  it seems like if you just put those  things down on a table they don't move  you see zero movement however the  individual particles in the solid are  moving they're moving very slowly but  they are moving most of the movement is  more of a vibrational movement and this  is simply because the atoms and the  bonds and the atoms they do have energy  with their vibrational energy in order  for the particles to have zero movements  to be completely still they have to  reach something called absolute zero  which is zero Kelvin on the Kelvin  temperature scale this is when movement  completely stops because there's no  energy left if you're interested in  learning more about the Kelvin  temperature scale please see my video on  converting between different temperature  scales however in most cases solids  still have some degree of movement even  though it's very very slow in fact  there's no place on earth that is as  cold as absolute zero so all solids on  earth are still going to be moving  somewhat even if it's just very slowly  now let's talk about proximity of  particles this means how near or far the  particles are kamichan in solids you can  see they're very very close together  so the various particles that are making  up solids are very close together they  often have a crystalline type of lattice  work that keeps them all very close  together gases on the other hand tends  to be very far apart so far apart that  they're only bouncing off of each other  occasionally and again liquid particles  tend to be in the middle of this range  where they are closer together than  solid particles excuse me they are  farther apart than solid particles but  closer together than gaseous particles  so that is it for these three states of  matter I hope you learned a lot and  thanks for watching biology put out  there
 

